const design={"schema":"chart-biopsy.integrated-overview.v1","nodes":{"assemble":{"id":"assemble","title":"Assemble the brief","subtitle":"Quotations and structured information","kind":"code","plain":"Code organizes accepted statements, quotations and existing structured records into the prepared chart brief.","technical":"Deterministically assemble the accepted extraction with source dates, units, attribution, uncertainties, conflicts and omissions. Keep validation/quarantine evidence.","input":"Validated extracted evidence and existing structured facts.","output":"Assembled brief ready for source checks.","boundary":"Readable organization still needs review. An evidence dump does not prove a useful clinical brief.","status":"Implemented and checked with invented software fixtures; extraction-arm hosted execution has not occurred."},"assess":{"id":"assess","title":"Separate AI rubric grader","subtitle":"Three provisional areas; no overall score","kind":"model","plain":"A separate AI grader uses the frozen rubric and saved case evidence to give provisional feedback on chart preparation, co-clinician intake, and the final assessment and plan. Grading requires both the frozen rubric and saved run evidence. It does not score the patient or the stand-in clinician. Human review can happen later.","technical":"Sol is default, separately selectable exact OpenAI/Anthropic grader identity in a fresh session. Inputs include eligible sources, accepted prepared brief/omissions, intake, handoff/alerts, both transcripts and final plan with stage/termination status. Current guidance is available.","input":"Frozen rubric, saved run evidence, eligible source projection and bounded source access.","output":"Provisional feedback in three areas, actual-opportunity evidence and unresolved rubric issues; distinguish encounter-caused gaps and patient non-disclosure from final-writer errors.","boundary":"Separate sessions prevent context leakage, not shared model blind spots. Masking is partial. Patient uncertainty and truncated evidence do not automatically prove a clinician omission. Separate observed non-disclosure from any claim about what the patient actually knew; available source-grant projections do not establish unspoken knowledge.","status":"Implemented locally and checked with invented fixtures. The default Sol authoring/grading route is blocked by provider credits; no live default Sol rubric or grade has been produced. Human rubric review remains pending."},"author":{"id":"author","title":"Author one shared patient packet","subtitle":"Chart-informed · independent of the method","kind":"model","plain":"Create one shared patient story and speaking profile from the eligible chart and authorized scenario. Use documented behavior to inform personality where possible; label uncertain details and inferences and simulation assumptions rather than guessing personality from demographics or diagnoses.","technical":"Author once from eligible case facts and the authorized scenario, without either prepared brief. Retain per-fact patient knowledge grants, behavioral quotes, uncertainty, contradictions and explicit assumptions; bind the same packet across both methods and draws. Current patient-author route: Gemini4 Argon (exact configured identifier gemini-4-argon); calls count in the shared Argon ledger; public-reference lookup is disabled; private eligible source lookup is bounded.","input":"Eligible case and authorized scenario facts.","output":"One exact version of the patient packet, with source/assumption provenance; the frozen comparison pins that version across methods and repeat runs.","boundary":"Demographics, diagnoses and medication lists alone do not establish personality. Hidden chart facts do not become patient memory.","status":"Implemented in the local research candidate. Live coverage varies by stage; software checks do not establish clinical correctness."},"caseReview":{"id":"caseReview","title":"Optional patient-packet review","subtitle":"Asynchronous inspection; no new approval gate","kind":"human","plain":"A person may inspect assumptions and uncertain patient knowledge, then record a new version with reasons if a change is needed.","technical":"Retain reviewer, reason, source/assumption provenance and original packet. Review status is explicit; this diagram does not require a new human approval before simulation.","input":"Selected patient packet and its source evidence.","output":"Review findings and optional separately versioned overrides for future use.","boundary":"Human review is not clinical approval. Changed packets must be identified rather than silently applied to already saved runs.","status":"Implemented locally. Human content adjudication, iPhone/device acceptance and installed-editor migration are not established. The existing editor and original human edits remain preserved."},"chart":{"id":"chart","title":"Preserve the chart","subtitle":"Original records and attachments","kind":"artifact","plain":"Keep the original chart available so that a reviewer can trace information back to where it came from.","technical":"Retain raw resources, decoded attachments, source IDs, hashes, locators, record status and distinct event/recording/availability dates.","input":"Selected chart export.","output":"Source-preserving inventory.","boundary":"Retaining a record does not establish that it is accurate, historically available, or relevant to this visit.","status":"Implemented in the local research candidate. Live coverage varies by stage; software checks do not establish clinical correctness."},"check":{"id":"check","title":"Check sources and boundaries","subtitle":"References, values and attribution","kind":"code","plain":"Check source references, numbers, dates, units and who said what. Keep rejected items and limitations visible.","technical":"Apply source binding, numeric/unit, ownership, temporal and schema validation. Preserve rejected material and trace its downstream availability. Distinguish item-level withholding/quarantine from whole-output failure, and preserve both. The exact treatment of rejected items in graded omission evidence must be documented before the comparison.","input":"Draft or assembled brief plus eligible sources.","output":"Checked output, limitations and retained failures.","boundary":"A linked quotation does not prove that the source supports the whole claim. Invalid or stopped preparation does not become a completed brief; failed attempts remain preserved.","status":"Implemented in the local research candidate. Live coverage varies by stage; software checks do not establish clinical correctness."},"checklist":{"id":"checklist","title":"50-topic checklist library","subtitle":"Eight modules; six or seven types each","kind":"artifact","plain":"Use a list of possible evaluation topics to guide attention, then select only those supported by this case.","technical":"All eight specialists collectively screen 50 element types. Each owns one six- or seven-item module; applicability, nonapplicability and unresolved reasons are recorded.","input":"Frozen generic topic catalog.","output":"Assigned module for each specialist.","boundary":"A checklist topic is not automatically an applicable criterion or proof of coverage.","status":"Development catalog, unreviewed. Topic source-support labels describe the generic catalog item, not its applicability to this case. They are not clinical approval."},"continuity":{"id":"continuity","title":"Patient-heard intake addendum","subtitle":"Actual speech; original packet unchanged","kind":"artifact","plain":"For the later conversation, carry forward what the patient actually heard and said during intake as a separate addendum.","technical":"Carry both speakers’ exact intake speech, including spoken suggestions as heard. Exclude host-only alerts and unspoken internal plans. Preserve the original packet; use a separate run-specific addendum for conversation two and for human practice when applicable.","input":"Exact spoken intake transcript; original patient packet remains unchanged.","output":"Patient continuity context and human SP addendum.","boundary":"Hearing a suggestion is not verified history, agreement or completed action.","status":"Actual intake speech was carried into the second conversation in the corrected invented confirmation; human SP continuity use remains unaccepted."},"direct":{"id":"direct","title":"Write a direct chart brief","subtitle":"Direct method adapted from the released tool","kind":"model","plain":"The workflow model writes a source-linked chart brief using released preparation infrastructure adapted to this task.","technical":"Run the adapted released direct preparation with high reasoning; preserve delivered input, raw responses, references and source checks.","input":"The same eligible source packet, structured chart records and visit task; no additional private chart-reader tool at this stage.","output":"Generated brief with cited sources.","boundary":"Adapted comparison means modified for this task. It does not establish equivalence to the old release or a quality advantage.","status":"Preparation completed in both invented hosted attempts. The first attempt failed later at the co-clinician handoff; that original failure is preserved."},"dossier":{"id":"dossier","title":"Saved dossier · all available stages","subtitle":"Save each artifact as it becomes available","kind":"artifact","plain":"Keep the chart preparation, patient packet, both conversations, handoff, final plan, failures, rubric versions and feedback in one readable case record. Store partial attempts and alerts even if later stages never start. Append human judgments, reveal history and new rubric versions without overwriting originals.","technical":"Store immutable source/case/run/rubric/grade artifacts as they become available. Append human judgments and recovery checkpoints separately. The first-judgment gate restricts display, not storage or generation.","input":"Case artifacts, traces, failures and review records.","output":"Readable persistent case workspace with private review recovery.","boundary":"Saved material is not clinical approval. Preserve partial/unstarted outcomes and the installed legacy editor; original artifacts are not overwritten.","status":"Implemented locally. Human content adjudication, iPhone/device acceptance and installed-editor migration are not established. The existing editor and original human edits remain preserved."},"earlierOutputs":{"id":"earlierOutputs","title":"Earlier saved case outputs","subtitle":"Preparation, intake, handoff and both transcripts","kind":"artifact","plain":"Bring forward the record from the preparation and two-conversation views, with sources, alerts and exact completion states.","technical":"Includes each method’s accepted prepared brief/references/omissions, intake, co-clinician handoff and host alerts, both transcripts, coverage and termination metadata. Final plan is supplied separately. The dossier also receives the shared patient packet and SP handouts; these are not automatically grading targets.","input":"Saved artifacts from views 02–05.","output":"Available stage evidence for grading and the dossier.","boundary":"Unavailable stages stay unavailable; truncation alone cannot establish an omission failure.","status":"Implemented in the local research candidate. Live coverage varies by stage; software checks do not establish clinical correctness."},"eligible":{"id":"eligible","title":"Select eligible chart evidence","subtitle":"Patient, event time and availability","kind":"code","plain":"Use records for this patient only when both the event and its documented availability are at or before the visit cutoff. Keep exclusions and unresolved dates visible.","technical":"Apply patient binding and known-by-both-times.v1. Unresolved required times, unreadable records, exclusions and context-budget omissions are accounted for; raw sources remain intact.","input":"Chart inventory and case definition.","output":"Eligible source registry and coverage record.","boundary":"An explicit timestamped visit cutoff is required. Historical event and availability limits are retained. Older frozen adapters use strict-before rules; exactly-at-cutoff sources can therefore be excluded, and the limitation is recorded. A boundary that cannot be represented is rejected rather than repaired with invented dates. Missing information never becomes a negative finding.","status":"Implemented in the local research candidate. Live coverage varies by stage; software checks do not establish clinical correctness."},"encounterRecord":{"id":"encounterRecord","title":"Saved clinician conversation","subtitle":"Second conversation · both speakers","kind":"artifact","plain":"Keep the clinician and patient speech from the second conversation exactly as it occurred.","technical":"Retain speaker identities, model/human origin, stage status and termination reason. A hard limit or provider failure preserves the partial record and stops dependent stages.","input":"Clinician and patient turns of the second conversation.","output":"Immutable complete or partial clinician-encounter transcript.","boundary":"An incomplete encounter does not produce a completed final plan. Keep encounter-attributable gaps separate from final-author errors.","status":"Retained the second conversation in the corrected invented confirmation. That stage did not start in the earlier failed attempt."},"extract":{"id":"extract","title":"Extract relevant chart statements","subtitle":"Extraction stage; may involve several recorded calls","kind":"model","plain":"The workflow model selects relevant statements and their supporting passages, preserving qualifications and uncertainty.","technical":"Medium-reasoning extraction retains raw candidates, accepted items and quarantined candidates. Code-owned validation precedes assembly; original structured records remain available.","input":"The same eligible source packet, structured chart records and visit task; no additional private chart-reader tool at this stage.","output":"Structured evidence candidates and exact references.","boundary":"This one preparation step may use multiple recorded tool/provider calls if configured. No call-saving or quality claim is implied.","status":"Implemented and checked with invented software fixtures; extraction-arm hosted execution has not occurred."},"failure":{"id":"failure","title":"Outcomes from every stage","subtitle":"Cross-cutting; preserve partial and unknown","kind":"artifact","plain":"Keep failed, malformed, partial, incomplete, unknown and unstarted outcomes instead of replacing them with a later success.","technical":"Hard limits or failed/unknown stages stop dependent stages and preserve exact partial transcripts. Independent safe work follows campaign pause rules. Tool continuations are retained calls within the same run condition. An unknown outcome pauses the affected campaign.","input":"Outcome evidence from any stage, not a step after the final plan.","output":"Explicit failure disposition and retained evidence.","boundary":"No unknown request is silently replayed. A corrected candidate has a separately bound identity; ordinary tool continuations do not each create a new experimental condition.","status":"Implemented in the local research candidate. Live coverage varies by stage; software checks do not establish clinical correctness."},"finalAssembly":{"id":"finalAssembly","title":"Code assembles and validates","subtitle":"Final text, coverage and complete contract","kind":"code","plain":"Code builds the final rubric from the model’s decisions and checks the complete result.","technical":"Deterministic assembly plus full criteria/rationale/notes/coverage/access/ownership validation. Preserve source hashes, failure evidence and all decision lineage.","input":"Reconciled keyed decisions and preserved source proposals.","output":"Structurally valid rubric, or retained invalid result.","boundary":"Structural validity is not semantic correctness or clinical approval.","status":"Implemented locally and checked with invented fixtures. The default Sol authoring/grading route is blocked by provider credits; no live default Sol rubric or grade has been produced. Human rubric review remains pending."},"finalap":{"id":"finalap","title":"Draft the final assessment and plan","subtitle":"Eligible chart + handoff + both transcripts","kind":"model","plain":"Write a draft from the eligible chart, the co-clinician handoff and both conversations. Separate ideas actually discussed from new post-encounter considerations.","technical":"Use preserved encounter-plan-v2 with source/actor/time/action evidence and optional current guidance. Distinguish discussion, patient response and proposed action; record uncertainty and limits. This final output is graded; the stand-in is not. Source projection records truncated/unindexed sources and context omissions; a bounded private reader provides original eligible source intervals.","input":"Eligible chart excerpts plus bounded private source lookup, handoff, and both exact transcripts.","output":"Final draft A/P with evidence and unresolved issues.","boundary":"No examination, executed order or treatment, final diagnosis, consent or clinical approval is inferred. Historical case facts and current guidance are separate timelines.","status":"Completed once in the corrected invented confirmation after both conversations. Did not start in the failed first attempt. Clinical/content adjudication remains pending. Bounded private lookup on a large chart was not exercised natively."},"floor":{"id":"floor","title":"Declare co-clinician task requirements","subtitle":"Expected behaviors; out-of-task duties flagged","kind":"artifact","plain":"State the minimum behaviors expected of the intake agent. Requirements outside that task are flagged before authoring, not silently dropped or lowered to fit performance.","technical":"Preserve declared clinician-only floor identity and scope edits. Apply recognized minimum correction before affected authoring; the stand-in clinician and patient are not grading targets. “Clinician-only floor” is a legacy identifier for declared co-clinician task minimums, not a score for the stand-in.","input":"Current case, capabilities and declared requirements.","output":"Sealed clinician-only requirement floor.","boundary":"Declared requirements are development inputs, not independently validated clinical truth. Do not turn patient duties into intake-agent criteria.","status":"Implemented locally and checked with invented fixtures. The default Sol authoring/grading route is blocked by provider credits; no live default Sol rubric or grade has been produced. Human rubric review remains pending."},"freeze":{"id":"freeze","title":"Freeze the shared rubric version","subtitle":"Before the grader sees saved outputs","kind":"code","plain":"Record the case rubric before grading either method, so both are judged against the same version.","technical":"Bind the case/source/task/floor/author/model/rubric and scoring policy before dispatch. Human review need not finish before provisional grading.","input":"Structurally valid case rubric and frozen protocol.","output":"One frozen rubric shared across both methods and repeat runs.","boundary":"Human review is asynchronous. All revisions applied to already saved runs are post-hoc for those runs, even if symmetric across both methods.","status":"Implemented locally and checked with invented fixtures. The default Sol authoring/grading route is blocked by provider credits; no live default Sol rubric or grade has been produced. Human rubric review remains pending."},"handoff":{"id":"handoff","title":"Prepared chart brief","subtitle":"For the co-clinician intake agent","kind":"artifact","plain":"Give the intake agent relevant chart history, source links, uncertainties and questions to clarify. This is different from the handoff written after intake.","technical":"Both methods produce an accepted prepared brief, references and explicit omissions. That common representation is the graded chart-preparation target; chart_extraction remains its schema ID.","input":"Accepted direct or code-assembled preparation.","output":"Prepared chart brief for intake and preparation-fidelity grading.","boundary":"The stand-in’s only case input is the separate co-clinician handoff, which may summarize this brief. The patient author is independent of either brief.","status":"Implemented in the local research candidate. Live coverage varies by stage; software checks do not establish clinical correctness."},"handoffDestinations":{"id":"handoffDestinations","title":"Continue to encounter and evaluation","subtitle":"Separate destinations; different information","kind":"artifact","plain":"The stand-in receives the co-clinician handoff only. The final-plan writer and grader receive the saved intake transcript as well as the handoff and alerts.","technical":"See Encounter & A/P for the second conversation and Review & report for separate three-area grading. The patient gets only patient-heard speech.","input":"Completed handoff, intake transcript and alerts.","output":"Distinct recipient projections in later views.","boundary":"A stopped intake retains a partial record and does not proceed as if completed.","status":"Implemented in the local research candidate. Live coverage varies by stage; software checks do not establish clinical correctness."},"initial":{"id":"initial","title":"Judge every run for the case","subtitle":"Separate run judgments · one case-wide gate","kind":"human","plain":"Save your first judgment for every run of this case before seeing any method labels or AI grades for any of them. Judge each run separately, including incomplete or failed attempts, and record readability, method guess and prior exposure.","technical":"Required target policy: bind the full case run roster and dossier hashes to this reviewer and case. The planned comparison has four runs per case. Require a saved initial judgment for every required current run; another reviewer’s judgment cannot unlock this case. Preserve append-only revisions, readability and exposure records.","input":"All required case run dossiers, with method labels, rubric/AI grades and raw condition identity hidden.","output":"Separate saved first judgments for every run, plus this reviewer’s case-wide completion state.","boundary":"A missing required run cannot silently be dropped. Prior exposure remains recorded and cannot be undone. The display gate never blocks simulation, grading or storage.","status":"Case-wide policy adopted by the user on 17 October. Plan and diagram updated; existing local review-server enforcement is still per-run and must be changed/tested before protocol use."},"intake":{"id":"intake","title":"Co-clinician intake","subtitle":"Updates, concerns and key checks","kind":"model","plain":"Before the main visit, ask what matters to the patient, gather relevant updates and check important chart details. Follow actual answers rather than a fixed questionnaire. The agent signals normal completion when it judges intake is finished; hard host limits mark an unfinished conversation as incomplete.","technical":"Argon is the workflow model. Focused history/context is default; broad intake is experimental. The host records a soft-limit event at 16 co-clinician turns per conversation, without a prompt change; at 24 it records incomplete and stops dependent stages. Optional current-reference search is enabled for the co-clinician; no original-chart reader is added.","input":"Prepared chart brief, task/capabilities and actual dialogue.","output":"Intake transcript, immediate alerts and completion state.","boundary":"No forced closing prompt, examination, autonomous order or final diagnosis. Rubric authoring and human review do not delay the conversation. No original-chart lookup beyond the accepted prepared brief. Alerts do not force a stop.","status":"Exercised in one corrected invented direct-method confirmation. The earlier malformed handoff remains preserved as a failed attempt. This is bounded engineering evidence, not clinical or study approval."},"integrator":{"id":"integrator","title":"Model integration decisions","subtitle":"Compact decisions referencing proposal IDs","kind":"model","plain":"A model chooses how the specialist proposals fit together and explains inclusion, exclusions and overlap ownership.","technical":"Return keyed selections/merges and dispositions without silently rewriting raw proposals. Preserve every excluded/unresolved item and useful note. Its bound context and proposals retain case-source grants; the configured author dispatcher supports optional public-reference lookup.","input":"All specialist outputs, ownership rules and case source grants.","output":"Model decision plan for deterministic preliminary assembly.","boundary":"Preserve failed/timeout attempts. Do not silently repair raw output or rewrite frozen V6 history.","status":"Implemented locally and checked with invented fixtures. The default Sol authoring/grading route is blocked by provider credits; no live default Sol rubric or grade has been produced. Human rubric review remains pending."},"memory":{"id":"memory","title":"Selected patient packet","subtitle":"Same bound version for simulation and SP","kind":"artifact","plain":"Keep one selected version of the patient facts, recollection and speaking style for both preparation methods and for human practice handouts. “Selected” means the exact version bound to a run, not an approval. The fixed comparison pins one version per case; later overrides do not mutate it.","technical":"Bind the authored packet version independently of preparation and retain source/assumption basis. Unknown personality settings remain null; moderate runtime defaults are labeled simulation assumptions.","input":"Authored patient packet and any explicitly versioned overrides.","output":"The same selected packet for the patient prompt and SP documents.","boundary":"Selected does not mean human-approved. New overrides create a new version; they do not edit the original packet or silently change a saved run.","status":"Implemented in the local research candidate. Live coverage varies by stage; software checks do not establish clinical correctness."},"minimum":{"id":"minimum","title":"Apply the minimum correction","subtitle":"test-purpose.minimum.v2","kind":"code","plain":"If the intake agent proposes relevant testing or result retrieval, require a purpose explanation and a check of the patient’s understanding of a concrete next step. If it makes no such proposal, this item does not apply.","technical":"Use the narrow recognized-rule revision before affected authoring. Preserve criterion identity and lineage. Explanation across turns counts; no proposal is N/A; omitted explanation is not double-penalized with its dependent check. The supplied minimum retains its SUPPLIED_MINIMUM owner. Matching checklist decisions can reference minimum_ids; they must not create a second overlapping purpose-explanation or dependent-check criterion. The integration record retains overlap ownership. Identity/group checks do not prove that all semantic duplication has been removed; human review remains needed. Old rubrics remain historical.","input":"Recognized legacy minimum requirements and authorized case sources.","output":"Corrected sealed floor and revision receipt.","boundary":"Keep the narrow minimum correction and original lineage. Patient unknown/declined recall does not automatically fail an appropriate check; do not double-penalize a dependent check after omitted teaching.","status":"Implemented locally and checked with invented fixtures. The default Sol authoring/grading route is blocked by provider credits; no live default Sol rubric or grade has been produced. Human rubric review remains pending."},"modelReconcile":{"id":"modelReconcile","title":"One model reconciles critiques","subtitle":"Every issue gets a disposition","kind":"model","plain":"One reconciler decides how to address the specialists’ critiques, keeping unresolved issues and disagreement visible.","technical":"Return a compact keyed reconciliation plan with every critique issue dispositioned. Team path: 8 authors + integrator + 8 critics + reconciler = 18 logical requests, before recorded tool continuations or corrections. Preserve original proposals and integration decisions; optional public-reference lookup follows the author dispatcher’s explicit configuration.","input":"Original proposals, integration lineage and eight critiques.","output":"Reconciliation decisions for final code assembly.","boundary":"Do not silently repair a failed original response. Logical requests and actual provider calls are separate denominators.","status":"Implemented locally and checked with invented fixtures. The default Sol authoring/grading route is blocked by provider credits; no live default Sol rubric or grade has been produced. Human rubric review remains pending."},"patient":{"id":"patient","title":"Same patient in both conversations","subtitle":"Simulation role; human practice alternative","kind":"model","plain":"The same patient takes part in the intake and later clinician conversation, answering naturally from the selected packet and the speech they actually heard.","technical":"The simulation uses the compiled V18 prompt and actual conversation turns only. Human practice uses the SP handout, records human-origin turns and makes zero model calls for those turns.","input":"Patient-known packet; actual current speech; patient-heard intake addendum for the second conversation.","output":"Patient replies with speaker identity.","boundary":"No clinician handoff, hidden chart, rubric or model-internal plan is given to the patient. The clinician rubric does not grade patient performance. A spoken suggestion is remembered as conversation, not verified history, agreement or completed action.","status":"The patient completed intake in both invented hosted attempts and the second conversation in the corrected confirmation. The first attempt stopped later at a malformed handoff. Human-practice/device acceptance and clinical approval remain pending."},"peers":{"id":"peers","title":"Eight original-context critiques","subtitle":"Specialists inspect the assembled rubric","kind":"model","plain":"Each specialist returns to its original case context and checks the combined rubric for missing, distorted or unfair criteria.","technical":"Use original specialist messages and output plus the preliminary rubric. Retain issue IDs, recommendations, useful notes and dissent. The critics receive no generated workflow outputs or grading feedback.","input":"Original specialist context and preliminary assembled rubric.","output":"Eight retained critique responses for one reconciler.","boundary":"Critique is model review, not clinical validation. It cannot replace human adjudication or remove shared Sol blind spots.","status":"Implemented locally and checked with invented fixtures. The default Sol authoring/grading route is blocked by provider credits; no live default Sol rubric or grade has been produced. Human rubric review remains pending."},"preliminary":{"id":"preliminary","title":"Code builds preliminary rubric","subtitle":"Fixed code assembles recorded decisions","kind":"code","plain":"Code assembles the selected proposal text and record of decisions into a preliminary rubric.","technical":"Apply the keyed integration plan, preserve excluded proposals and notes, and validate required structure before critique.","input":"Raw specialist proposals and integration decisions.","output":"Preliminary assembled rubric with full record of decisions.","boundary":"Assembly follows decisions; it does not substitute a new model-written rubric or erase dissent.","status":"Implemented locally and checked with invented fixtures. The default Sol authoring/grading route is blocked by provider credits; no live default Sol rubric or grade has been produced. Human rubric review remains pending."},"references":{"id":"references","title":"Optional current reference lookup","subtitle":"Approved nonidentifying questions","kind":"code","plain":"Agents may ask for current guidance when useful. Preserve the question, dated source passage and how it affected reasoning; keep unavailable or conflicting guidance visible.","technical":"The operator supplies a versioned approved medical-vocabulary list before runs. Restricted bounded search/read rejects raw chart text and identifiers. Tool rejection/unavailability is returned to the agent; invalid final output or exhausted model steps stops dependents.","input":"Agent-selected question using approved nonidentifying clinical terms.","output":"Separate saved query, passage, date, usage and unavailable/conflicting-result records, stored alongside dialogue; unspoken tool logs never become transcript speech.","boundary":"Current best practice is the intended development standard; the chart cutoff limits case facts, not reference publication dates. Workflow-model tool continuations count in the Argon ceiling; author/grader lookups use separate ledgers.","status":"One public search completed in the invented confirmation. Native passage reads and author/grader lookups were not exercised; graceful public-reference I/O-failure handling still needs hardening."},"regradeQueue":{"id":"regradeQueue","title":"Queue both saved methods","subtitle":"Pending work; originals retained","kind":"code","plain":"Queue affected saved runs from both methods for grading against an explicitly finalized new rubric version. This is pending work, not automatic model dispatch; old feedback remains unchanged.","technical":"Keep original grades and hashes. The queue is pending work, not an automatic provider dispatch or a second comparison campaign. Any eventual dispatch needs explicit operator action on a qualified route and is recorded in the separate author/grader accounting; the queue itself makes no provider call.","input":"Finalized human version and affected saved run/grade identities.","output":"Pending regrade jobs in both arms, with prior grades retained.","boundary":"No regrade happens on each keystroke. Revisions to saved runs are post-hoc.","status":"Implemented locally and checked with invented fixtures. The default Sol authoring/grading route is blocked by provider credits; no live default Sol rubric or grade has been produced. Human rubric review remains pending."},"report":{"id":"report","title":"Research report and remaining work","subtitle":"Same facts; three reading focuses","kind":"artifact","plain":"Explain changes, impacts, provisional model findings, human judgments, retained disagreement and what remains unresolved. Human handoff readability is a separate qualitative finding. Later revisions are changes made after seeing saved results; retain those separately from original feedback.","technical":"Clinical/engineering/presentation focuses use the same saved facts. Include stage denominators, calls/time/usage, provisional original-rubric results first, separately labeled post-hoc revisions, failures, dissent, remediation and risk. Separate encounter-caused gaps and patient non-disclosure from final-author errors. Include coverage, missing usage, complete/failed/unknown/incomplete/not-started slots, and unresolved rubric items.","input":"Saved dossiers, run receipts, review records and exact qualification status.","output":"Readable research report plus remediation backlog.","boundary":"40 runs / 80 conversations arise from 10 reused cases. Results are descriptive development evidence, not 40 independent cases, clinical usability, general superiority or clinical/study approval.","status":"Implemented in the local research candidate. Live coverage varies by stage; software checks do not establish clinical correctness."},"reveal":{"id":"reveal","title":"Reveal only after all case runs are judged","subtitle":"Case-wide display gate for this reviewer","kind":"code","plain":"Only after you have judged every required run for the case can the interface reveal its method labels and available AI grades. A later grade may appear when available; generation and storage do not wait for your review.","technical":"No run in the case may expose a method label or AI grade until every required bound run has this reviewer’s saved first judgment. Verify the complete roster and dossier hashes before revealing; do not unlock a partial catalog. Save a case-wide exposure event and preserve earlier exposures.","input":"Complete required case roster, all of this reviewer’s first judgments, and available saved method/rubric/grade records.","output":"Case-wide reveal permission and durable exposure evidence for this reviewer.","boundary":"Per-run reveal is prohibited under the adopted policy. A roster change requires new completion/binding checks; historical exposure is never erased.","status":"Case-wide policy adopted by the user on 17 October. Plan and diagram updated; existing local review-server enforcement is still per-run and must be changed/tested before protocol use."},"revise":{"id":"revise","title":"Finalize a new human rubric version","subtitle":"Post-hoc for already saved runs","kind":"human","plain":"If a person finalizes changes, keep the original rubric and feedback, record the new version, and queue affected saved runs from both methods for regrading.","technical":"Finalized reviewer/reason/version lineage queues both arms, retaining old grades and author/reviewer origin. An annotation or keystroke does not dispatch a provider or finalize a rubric.","input":"Human rubric edits, old rubric and saved outputs.","output":"New rubric version, review lineage and regrade queue.","boundary":"Symmetric regrading does not make an after-exposure edit prospective. Original feedback and revised feedback remain separate.","status":"Implemented locally. Human content adjudication, iPhone/device acceptance and installed-editor migration are not established. The existing editor and original human edits remain preserved."},"route":{"id":"route","title":"Assign the preparation method","subtitle":"Two whole-method comparison conditions","kind":"code","plain":"Compare two ways to prepare the same chart for the intake agent. Repeat preparation for every run; use the same independently authored patient packet.","technical":"Direct adapts released source-linked preparation with high reasoning. Extraction uses medium reasoning plus code assembly. Compare the whole packages; do not attribute a difference to reasoning or extraction alone.","input":"Eligible chart, task and frozen configuration.","output":"One named preparation condition per run.","boundary":"Neither method is assumed better. Both use the same patient template (V18), so this comparison cannot identify that template’s effect. Patient-authoring calls and provenance are recorded separately.","status":"Implemented in the local research candidate. Live coverage varies by stage; software checks do not establish clinical correctness."},"rubric":{"id":"rubric","title":"Shared frozen case rubric","subtitle":"One frozen version per case and authoring condition","kind":"artifact","plain":"An AI specialist team writes one case-specific rubric for chart preparation, intake including the handoff, and the final plan. Both methods and both repeat runs use the same frozen version.","technical":"chart_extraction retains its schema name but assesses each method’s accepted prepared brief, references and omissions. coclinician_intake includes the exchange, handoff and host alerts. No overall score; patient and stand-in are evidence only. The declared comparison uses the same chosen case rubric across both methods and both repeat runs. Single-author or alternate-model experiments have separate condition identities and cannot silently replace it. Each specialist may propose criteria for any of the three named targets when supported by its assigned topic and case sources. Chart-preparation criteria check relevant original chart details are retained and represented faithfully; intake criteria check the co-clinician’s actual exchange/handoff; final_ap criteria check the written final plan. Generic catalog “clinician” wording never scores the stand-in. A behavior available only in conversation two is context for the final plan or not applicable, never a retroactive intake duty.","input":"Final deterministic assembly and full structural validation.","output":"One provisional case rubric with criteria, rationale, notes and lineage.","boundary":"Human-unreviewed rubrics remain provisional. Structural, semantic, human and clinical approval states are distinct.","status":"Implemented locally and checked with invented fixtures. The default Sol authoring/grading route is blocked by provider credits; no live default Sol rubric or grade has been produced. Human rubric review remains pending."},"scenario":{"id":"scenario","title":"Source-bound case scenario and task","subtitle":"Source-bound facts; no generated answers","kind":"artifact","plain":"The shared task, authorized patient-known source passages and exact scenario facts establish the case before any encounter runs. They are shared by the authoring condition used for both preparation methods.","technical":"Use authorized original case/scenario and source grants, independently of prepared briefs and generated dialogue. This is not a generated handoff or a runtime answer key. Later, for rubric authors (see Rubric team), the converter validates the exact shared patient packet, then projects authorized source spans and exact scenario.patient_facts. It does not send the full generated personality packet as an answer key. Assumptions retain their origin. Permitted authored assumptions must exactly match authorized scenario.patient_facts and remain scenario-added sources, distinct from original chart passages.","input":"Frozen case/scenario facts and actor access.","output":"Common authorized source context for patient and rubric authoring.","boundary":"No generated preparation, conversations, final plans, prior rubrics or examiner answers enter fresh author input. Source-projected patient facts and explicitly authorized scenario facts are distinct from run outputs. Answer-derived notes are excluded. Exact authorized scenario additions may enter with their scenario origin; packet-only personality defaults or inferences do not become clinical source facts.","status":"Implemented in the local research candidate. Live coverage varies by stage; software checks do not establish clinical correctness."},"semantic":{"id":"semantic","title":"Asynchronous human rubric review","subtitle":"Meaning, fairness and unresolved issues","kind":"human","plain":"A person checks whether the rubric is fair, useful and supported, while provisional grading can already proceed. A rubric-only reviewer can inspect the instrument independently of the dossier-first workflow. If that reviewer later judges a dossier, record prior exposure; this role policy needs explicit documentation before the comparison.","technical":"Inspect the frozen provisional rubric, sources, alternatives, overlap and uncertainty. Save reviewer/reason and exposure state. This node does not introduce an Opus-assisted authoring stage or a new approval gate. Grader-flagged issues are saved evidence for human inspection; seeing them is exposure, not a fresh blinded judgment.","input":"Frozen provisional rubric and authorized review evidence.","output":"Retained unresolved findings or explicit finalized human revision.","boundary":"Not clinical approval. Any revision applied to saved runs is post-hoc; keep original frozen-rubric feedback first.","status":"Implemented locally and checked with invented fixtures. The default Sol authoring/grading route is blocked by provider credits; no live default Sol rubric or grade has been produced. Human rubric review remains pending."},"sp":{"id":"sp","title":"Standardized-patient (SP) handouts","subtitle":"The same selected patient packet","kind":"artifact","plain":"Give a standardized patient (SP)—a trained person playing the patient—the facts and portrayal guidance needed to act consistently and naturally.","technical":"Derive opening, history, relevant positives/negatives, concerns, portrayal, disclosure and unknown handling from the same patient packet. Export quick/full HTML, PDF through browser print, and a full-briefing DOCX; continuity addendum includes only actual prior speech.","input":"The same selected patient packet; optional patient-heard intake addendum.","output":"SP quick sheet and full briefing in print HTML (PDF via browser print); editable full briefing in DOCX; optional patient-heard continuity addendum.","boundary":"Human practice is outside the fixed model comparison. No hidden clinical plan or rubric answers; a portrayal guide is not a script to memorize.","status":"Implemented locally. Human content adjudication, iPhone/device acceptance and installed-editor migration are not established. The existing editor and original human edits remain preserved."},"specialists":{"id":"specialists","title":"Eight rubric-writing specialists","subtitle":"Same case; collectively screen 50 types","kind":"model","plain":"Eight specialist agents each receive the case and a different checklist of possible evaluation topics. Each selects the topics that matter for this case, writes case-specific criteria, and explains what it included, excluded or could not resolve. Every proposed criterion names its target: prepared chart brief, co-clinician intake/handoff, or written final assessment and plan. The clinician stand-in and patient are never scored.","technical":"Same eligible case/scenario/task/minimums for all; six or seven assigned types per specialist, with applicability/reasons retained. Sol is default in every authoring stage; exact OpenAI/Anthropic alternatives are explicit. Current references are optional.","input":"Eligible chart, source-projected authorized patient/scenario facts, task requirements, assigned checklist module and optional current guidance; no generated run outputs or full personality packet answer key.","output":"Eight proposal sets with rationale, notes, applicability and ownership.","boundary":"Fresh authors never see prepared briefs, generated conversations/plans, previous rubrics or examiner answers. Team agreement and same-model sessions are not independent clinical validation. No answer-derived notes enter author inputs. The expanded parent box represents the team, not a ninth model call.","status":"Implemented locally and checked with invented fixtures. The default Sol authoring/grading route is blocked by provider credits; no live default Sol rubric or grade has been produced. Human rubric review remains pending."},"standin":{"id":"standin","title":"Separate clinician conversation","subtitle":"Simple simulation; future human role","kind":"model","plain":"A simple clinician simulation talks with the same patient, starting from the co-clinician handoff. A human clinician can later take this role.","technical":"Case data input is the co-clinician handoff only, plus its own conversation. Current reference lookup is off by default and may be enabled as an explicit condition. Host-only soft/hard limits apply; a stopped conversation remains incomplete. This displayed condition always excludes original chart and intake-transcript access. Host limits are soft 16 and hard 24 clinician turns per conversation. The patient may repeat actual intake speech; that is conversational disclosure, not a hidden transcript feed.","input":"Co-clinician handoff only (written after intake; see Intake view).","output":"Separate clinician-patient transcript.","boundary":"No original chart or intake transcript is given to this actor. It is not a clinician-rubric target, though its behavior can affect the final plan’s available evidence.","status":"Ran once for two exchanges in the corrected invented confirmation. Did not start in the failed first attempt. Not clinically approved."},"summary":{"id":"summary","title":"Co-clinician handoff","subtitle":"After intake · only case input to stand-in","kind":"model","plain":"Give the clinician the key positive and elicited negative details, updates, concerns, chart/patient discrepancies, unknowns, alerts and a tentative assessment and plan.","technical":"Use the prepared chart brief plus exact intake evidence. Negatives need actual patient evidence; retain actor/time/action state. Code inserts recorded host alerts. Handoff content and alerts are included in the intake grading target. Written by a separate Argon handoff stage after intake; optional public references are enabled. Original chart access is limited to accepted prepared-brief source excerpts.","input":"Prepared chart brief, completed intake transcript and retained alerts.","output":"Evidence-linked co-clinician handoff for the stand-in, final plan, grading and dossier.","boundary":"An unasked question is not a negative finding. A suggested plan is not patient agreement or completed care.","status":"Exercised in one corrected invented direct-method confirmation. The earlier malformed handoff remains preserved as a failed attempt. This is bounded engineering evidence, not clinical or study approval. Pointer-only/structured-source evidence coverage at handoff remains a hardening gap."},"transcript":{"id":"transcript","title":"Saved intake conversation","subtitle":"First conversation · both speakers","kind":"artifact","plain":"Save every co-clinician and patient turn exactly, including an incomplete or failed intake.","technical":"Preserve immutable speaker-labeled turns, prompt/request provenance, model settings, terminal reasons and failure evidence. Keep auxiliary unspoken plans separate. Queries and host alerts are saved as adjacent run artifacts, never inserted into exact spoken turns.","input":"Actual co-clinician and patient intake speech.","output":"Immutable complete or partial intake transcript with termination state.","boundary":"A clinician statement is not patient confirmation. A partial transcript is retained; stopped intake does not fabricate a downstream handoff or final plan.","status":"Exact intake turns were retained in both invented hosted attempts. Unspoken alerts and reference logs stay in separate run artifacts."},"urgent":{"id":"urgent","title":"Show alerts immediately","subtitle":"Keep talking; highlight in handoff","kind":"code","plain":"Make a concern reported by the intake model visible immediately in the host record, and preserve it prominently in the completed handoff. Intake may continue.","technical":"The intake model reports alerts; host code emits and preserves them, and inserts retained alerts even if the handoff model omits one. A missed concern is not made detectable by this mechanism.","input":"Actual conversation and recorded concerns.","output":"Immediate visible alert and separate saved host-event evidence, retained even if intake stops before a handoff.","boundary":"No universal emergency detector or autonomous treatment is implied. Alerts do not force an interruption; failures and hard limits do stop dependent stages.","status":"Implemented in the local research candidate. Live coverage varies by stage; software checks do not establish clinical correctness."},"v18":{"id":"v18","title":"Render the kept patient voice","subtitle":"V18 template · personality and language","kind":"code","plain":"Turn the selected packet into the kept V18 patient prompt: six personality traits plus English-language level. V18 names the previously tested voice template being reused.","technical":"Preserve the exact V18 role and H/E/X/A/C/O/CEFR descriptors in the new compiler adapter. Unknown source settings remain recorded as unknown; the runtime uses separately labeled simulation-assumption defaults. Any harmless everyday cosmetic detail allowed by the kept template cannot introduce medical facts. Record this compiler adapter as its own condition; it is not the original V18 experimental condition.","input":"Authorized patient memory, encounter date and portrayal settings.","output":"Actual V18 system prompt and provenance.","boundary":"Reuse of the voice template does not qualify the combined workflow. Do not invent diagnoses, results, exposures or definite unlisted negatives.","status":"Implemented in the local research candidate. Live coverage varies by stage; software checks do not establish clinical correctness."},"prepareOverview":{"id":"prepareOverview","title":"Prepare chart by one of two methods","subtitle":"Adapted direct or extraction plus code","kind":"model","plain":"For each run, the workflow model prepares the chart by the selected direct or extraction-plus-code method. The accepted brief is used by intake and is assessed separately.","technical":"Direct adapts released source-linked preparation with high reasoning. Extraction uses medium reasoning plus code assembly. Compare the whole packages; do not attribute a difference to reasoning or extraction alone. This overview node summarizes the model and code steps expanded in Chart preparation.","input":"Eligible chart, task and frozen configuration.","output":"Accepted chart brief with references and explicit omissions, under the assigned preparation condition.","boundary":"Neither method is assumed better. Both use the same patient template (V18), so this comparison cannot identify that template’s effect. Patient-authoring calls and provenance are recorded separately.","status":"Direct preparation completed in both invented hosted attempts; extraction has invented software checks but no hosted execution yet."},"specialist_01":{"id":"specialist_01","title":"History & Context Elicitation","subtitle":"MOD-01 · 6 assigned screening topics","kind":"model","plain":"This specialist checks symptoms, relevant history, medicines, warning signs and practical context. It selects topics that matter for this case and turns them into specific evaluation criteria. Topics that do not apply, or remain uncertain, need reasons.","technical":"Clinician inquiry into presenting symptoms, competing differentials, organ/systemic red flags, medication/allergy/preventive/ophthalmic history, longitudinal data discrepancies, exposures, functional/access/self-management context, and collateral history. Exact catalog questions below are candidate element types, not automatic scored criteria. All eight collectively screen 50 types. Keep applicability, inclusion rationale, excluded proposals, overlap ownership and unresolved facets.","input":"The same eligible chart, source-bound authorized scenario, task and corrected minimums; this specialist’s assigned checklist module; optional current guidance.","output":"Case-specific proposals, source rationale, notes and applicability decisions for the integrator.","status":"Implemented locally and checked with invented fixtures. The default Sol authoring/grading route is blocked by provider credits; no live default Sol rubric or grade has been produced. Human rubric review remains pending.","boundary":"Generic topics can describe acts outside this encounter’s capabilities. Adapt only to the declared task; do not add dummy actor criteria, require unavailable actions, score the patient or stand-in, or treat template examples as clinical approval. Test-purpose.minimum.v2 remains a distinct pre-authoring correction. The retained supplied minimum owns its protected requirement; use minimum_ids for matching checklist coverage instead of a duplicate criterion.","topics":[{"id":"M1-I1","question":"Does the clinician characterize the primary symptom pattern and probe competing syndromic differentials beyond the volunteered complaint?","include_when":"The presentation involves undifferentiated, overlapping, or self-framed symptoms where anchoring on the opening complaint risks missing a primary or coexisting etiology.","exclude_when":"The diagnosis is already established and the encounter focuses solely on stable follow-up or a targeted procedural transition without new symptoms.","adequate":"Clinician systematically asks about symptom onset, quality, pattern, and differentiating features across plausible syndromic alternatives.","insufficient":"Clinician accepts the patient's initial framing without probing differentiating symptom clusters.","basis":"unresolved_source_support"},{"id":"M1-I2","question":"Does the clinician actively screen for acute, systemic, or organ-specific red-flag symptoms during history-taking?","include_when":"The presenting complaint or clinical trajectory carries a plausible risk of life-, organ-, vision-, or psychiatric safety-threatening complications.","exclude_when":"No plausible red-flag syndrome applies to the presenting problem or visit stage.","adequate":"Clinician explicitly inquires about relevant warning symptoms for the affected system (including self-harm or safety risk where indicated) during assessment.","insufficient":"Clinician omits red-flag inquiry and relies on the patient to volunteer alarm symptoms unprompted.","basis":"unresolved_source_support"},{"id":"M1-I3","question":"Does the clinician elicit or re-verify relevant prescription drugs, over-the-counter products, supplements, allergies, and pertinent preventive or ophthalmic history?","include_when":"Diagnostic evaluation, safe prescribing, or preventive care depends on current medications, self-medication, administration habits, allergies, immunization/screening status, or baseline eye/vision history.","exclude_when":"Medication, allergy, and relevant preventive/ophthalmic histories are already verified for the current decision and unchanged.","adequate":"Clinician proactively asks about relevant prescribed and non-prescribed agents, allergies, and pertinent preventive or ophthalmic history before making dependent clinical decisions, accepting unknown answers if unscripted.","insufficient":"Clinician prescribes or interprets findings without checking relevant concurrent agents, allergies, or required baseline preventive/ophthalmic history.","basis":"design_extension"},{"id":"M1-I4","question":"Does the clinician nonjudgmentally probe and reconcile discrepancies between objective findings and reported history or adherence?","include_when":"Returned test results, vital signs, or symptom trajectory conflict with the patient's prior statements, self-reported diagnosis, or expected treatment response.","exclude_when":"History, reported adherence, and objective findings are concordant across visits.","adequate":"Clinician identifies the contradiction and asks targeted questions about interval habits, pre-test actions, or prior records to clarify the clinical picture (scored on the clinician's inquiry, regardless of patient disclosure).","insufficient":"Clinician ignores the discrepancy, orders invasive workup for an unverified claim, or labels treatment a failure without inquiring about confounders.","basis":"unresolved_source_support"},{"id":"M1-I5","question":"Does the clinician inquire about relevant occupational hazards, environmental triggers, travel/infectious exposures, substance use, or family history?","include_when":"Etiology, risk stratification, or management depends on workplace irritants/ergonomics, household allergens, travel or dietary exposures, tobacco/alcohol/substance use, or hereditary disease patterns.","exclude_when":"Occupational, environmental, infectious, substance, and familial factors have no bearing on the clinical problem.","adequate":"Clinician asks targeted questions regarding the applicable exposure, substance quantification, or family history domain.","insufficient":"Clinician focuses narrowly on symptoms and omits exposure, substance, or familial risk inquiry supported by the case.","basis":"workbook_exemplar"},{"id":"M1-I6","question":"Does the clinician proactively inquire about functional/daily-living impact, healthcare access or self-management barriers, social support, and collateral history (with appropriate consent)?","include_when":"Symptoms or proposed care may affect work, daily function, or independent living; logistics/access or self-management capacity may constrain care; or a witness/caregiver is involved or insight is impaired.","exclude_when":"The encounter is a straightforward solo visit where functional impact, access, support, and collateral history are already clear and uninvolved.","adequate":"Clinician proactively asks how symptoms affect daily function/work, checks practical access/self-management capability and support (accepting unknown answers when unscripted), and in triadic visits integrates collateral history while respecting patient engagement and confidentiality/consent.","insufficient":"Clinician omits functional, access, or support inquiry when relevant, accepts anosognosic minimization without exploring collateral context, or shares/solicits third-party information without addressing consent.","basis":"design_extension"}]},"specialist_02":{"id":"specialist_02","title":"Examination & Diagnostic Stewardship","subtitle":"MOD-02 · 6 assigned screening topics","kind":"model","plain":"This specialist checks examination needs, diagnostic testing and safe use of investigations. It selects topics that matter for this case and turns them into specific evaluation criteria. Topics that do not apply, or remain uncertain, need reasons.","technical":"Targeted physical examination and vital signs, actionable exam deferral or modality coordination, clinical probability/severity scoring, confirmatory and exclusion test ordering, pre-analytical patient preparation instructions, pre-treatment safety labs, and diagnostic stewardship. Exact catalog questions below are candidate element types, not automatic scored criteria. All eight collectively screen 50 types. Keep applicability, inclusion rationale, excluded proposals, overlap ownership and unresolved facets.","input":"The same eligible chart, source-bound authorized scenario, task and corrected minimums; this specialist’s assigned checklist module; optional current guidance.","output":"Case-specific proposals, source rationale, notes and applicability decisions for the integrator.","status":"Implemented locally and checked with invented fixtures. The default Sol authoring/grading route is blocked by provider credits; no live default Sol rubric or grade has been produced. Human rubric review remains pending.","boundary":"Generic topics can describe acts outside this encounter’s capabilities. Adapt only to the declared task; do not add dummy actor criteria, require unavailable actions, score the patient or stand-in, or treat template examples as clinical approval. Test-purpose.minimum.v2 remains a distinct pre-authoring correction. The retained supplied minimum owns its protected requirement; use minimum_ids for matching checklist coverage instead of a duplicate criterion.","topics":[{"id":"M2-I1","question":"Does the clinician perform or arrange required targeted physical examinations and vital sign assessments, or execute an actionable exam deferral?","include_when":"Clinical assessment requires specific bedside measurements, focused organ examination, virtual-to-in-person exam coordination, or context-sensitive deferral of an intimate or contraindicated maneuver.","exclude_when":"No physical examination maneuver or modality transition is clinically relevant at the visit.","adequate":"Clinician performs or requests the specific examination parameters, or when deferral/modality change is needed, explains why and arranges a concrete, actionable plan to complete the assessment safely.","insufficient":"Clinician omits essential physical findings, performs a contraindicated maneuver, or defers an exam without arranging an actionable follow-through plan.","basis":"workbook_exemplar"},{"id":"M2-I2","question":"Does the clinician assess parameters for and apply a relevant clinical probability, severity, or symptom-burden tool?","include_when":"Case context supports structured risk stratification to guide disposition, diagnostic testing, or treatment intensity.","exclude_when":"No structured clinical scoring or risk-stratification framework applies to the decision point.","adequate":"Clinician evaluates the constituent clinical parameters and applies the structured risk or severity assessment to inform management.","insufficient":"Clinician makes disposition or testing decisions without assessing severity parameters or risk probability.","basis":"workbook_exemplar"},{"id":"M2-I3","question":"Does the clinician order appropriate confirmatory, secondary-cause exclusion, or staged diagnostic investigations?","include_when":"Establishing the diagnosis, ruling out reversible mimics, or resolving equivocal baseline findings requires laboratory, microbiological, physiological, or imaging orders.","exclude_when":"Diagnostic evaluation is already complete and no further diagnostic or staging investigations are indicated.","adequate":"Clinician orders the case-supported confirmatory and exclusion investigations at the appropriate diagnostic stage.","insufficient":"Clinician initiates long-term disease-directed treatment without required diagnostic confirmation, omits secondary-cause exclusion testing, or fails to escalate staged testing when baseline results are inconclusive.","basis":"unresolved_source_support"},{"id":"M2-I4","question":"Does the clinician provide necessary pre-analytical patient preparation or specimen sequencing instructions when ordering tests?","include_when":"Test validity depends on patient preparation prior to sampling (such as medication holds, dietary continuation/fasting, or collecting cultures before starting antimicrobials).","exclude_when":"Ordered investigations require no special patient preparation or sequencing.","adequate":"Clinician clearly instructs the patient on required dietary, medication, or timing preparation before specimen collection or testing.","insufficient":"Clinician orders a preparation-sensitive investigation without instructing the patient on pre-test requirements.","basis":"unresolved_source_support"},{"id":"M2-I5","question":"Does the clinician practice diagnostic stewardship by withholding unindicated or duplicate tests and providing an actionable deferral explanation?","include_when":"A test is requested by the patient or commonly overused but is not clinically indicated at the current stage, or recent valid results already exist.","exclude_when":"All discussed or requested investigations are clinically indicated at the current visit.","adequate":"Clinician refrains from ordering the unnecessary or duplicate test, explains why it is not currently helpful, and outlines actionable clinical contingencies that would prompt future testing.","insufficient":"Clinician orders unindicated testing defensively or refuses a requested test abruptly without explaining the rationale and reassessment criteria.","basis":"unresolved_source_support"},{"id":"M2-I6","question":"Does the clinician order required baseline organ-function or safety-clearance investigations before starting high-risk therapy?","include_when":"Planned pharmacotherapy or biological treatment requires pre-treatment organ function, metabolic, hematologic, infectious, or cardiac clearance.","exclude_when":"The selected therapy requires no specific pre-treatment baseline laboratory or clearance testing.","adequate":"Clinician orders the necessary baseline safety or clearance investigations prior to or concurrently with treatment initiation.","insufficient":"Clinician starts high-risk pharmacotherapy without checking required baseline safety parameters.","basis":"unresolved_source_support"}]},"specialist_03":{"id":"specialist_03","title":"Diagnostic & Prognostic Communication","subtitle":"MOD-03 · 6 assigned screening topics","kind":"model","plain":"This specialist checks how tests are explained: tests, results, uncertainty, prognosis and hereditary risk. It selects topics that matter for this case and turns them into specific evaluation criteria. Topics that do not apply, or remain uncertain, need reasons.","technical":"Independently explaining the purpose of ordered investigations; interpreting returned lab, imaging, pathology, and discharge results; clarifying symptom attribution and misconceptions; communicating diagnostic uncertainty; disclosing serious news and realistic trajectories; and explaining hereditary risk. Exact catalog questions below are candidate element types, not automatic scored criteria. All eight collectively screen 50 types. Keep applicability, inclusion rationale, excluded proposals, overlap ownership and unresolved facets.","input":"The same eligible chart, source-bound authorized scenario, task and corrected minimums; this specialist’s assigned checklist module; optional current guidance.","output":"Case-specific proposals, source rationale, notes and applicability decisions for the integrator.","status":"Implemented locally and checked with invented fixtures. The default Sol authoring/grading route is blocked by provider credits; no live default Sol rubric or grade has been produced. Human rubric review remains pending.","boundary":"Generic topics can describe acts outside this encounter’s capabilities. Adapt only to the declared task; do not add dummy actor criteria, require unavailable actions, score the patient or stand-in, or treat template examples as clinical approval. Test-purpose.minimum.v2 remains a distinct pre-authoring correction. The retained supplied minimum owns its protected requirement; use minimum_ids for matching checklist coverage instead of a duplicate criterion.","topics":[{"id":"M3-I1","question":"Does the clinician independently explain the clinical purpose of ordered investigations and what the patient should expect, regardless of whether the patient asks?","include_when":"The clinician orders diagnostic, staging, or baseline safety investigations during the encounter.","exclude_when":"No investigations are ordered at the visit.","adequate":"Clinician proactively explains in plain language why each test or panel is being ordered, what question it answers, and what the procedure involves, without waiting for the patient to prompt.","insufficient":"Clinician merely names or orders tests without explaining their diagnostic or safety purpose to the patient.","basis":"design_extension"},{"id":"M3-I2","question":"Does the clinician interpret and explain returned laboratory, imaging, pathology, or hospital discharge findings in plain language?","include_when":"New investigation reports or emergency/inpatient discharge summaries are available for review at the visit.","exclude_when":"No new diagnostic results or discharge summaries are present at the visit.","adequate":"Clinician walks through relevant abnormal and reassuringly normal findings and explains how they establish the diagnosis, disease stage, or clinical stability.","insufficient":"Clinician states only a diagnostic label without explaining the underlying test or discharge findings, or merely promises to review them later.","basis":"unresolved_source_support"},{"id":"M3-I3","question":"Does the clinician clarify symptom attribution, correct causal misconceptions, and provide evidence-based reassurance where serious pathology is excluded?","include_when":"The patient misattributes symptoms to an unrelated condition or medication, or expresses fear of malignancy, organ failure, or permanent harm.","exclude_when":"Symptom attribution is straightforward and no misconception or illness fear is present.","adequate":"Clinician directly addresses the patient's attribution belief or fear, explains the actual physiological cause of the symptoms, and reassures based on objective findings.","insufficient":"Clinician ignores the patient's causal misconception or fear, leaving anxiety or false attribution unaddressed.","basis":"unresolved_source_support"},{"id":"M3-I4","question":"Does the clinician communicate diagnostic uncertainty and explain the rationale for a provisional diagnosis or staged exclusion pathway?","include_when":"Initial findings are equivocal, normal between episodes, or discordant with clinical suspicion, requiring longitudinal observation or staged testing.","exclude_when":"Available findings establish a definitive, unambiguous diagnosis.","adequate":"Clinician explains why current results do not yet provide final certainty and outlines the logical next diagnostic or monitoring step without premature labeling.","insufficient":"Clinician asserts an unsupported definitive diagnosis on equivocal data or falsely rules out an episodic condition based on a normal baseline test.","basis":"unresolved_source_support"},{"id":"M3-I5","question":"Does the clinician disclose serious or chronic diagnoses empathetically and set realistic expectations for disease trajectory, control versus cure, or recovery timelines?","include_when":"The patient receives a serious, progressive, or chronic diagnosis, or asks about curability, long-term prognosis, or exact recovery dates.","exclude_when":"The condition is minor and self-limiting and the patient holds realistic recovery expectations.","adequate":"Clinician delivers diagnostic and prognostic information sensitively, clarifies ongoing disease control versus cure, and frames expected recovery or progression timelines honestly without false guarantees.","insufficient":"Clinician delivers serious news bluntly, promises an unrealistic cure or fixed recovery date, or evades direct prognostic questions.","basis":"workbook_exemplar"},{"id":"M3-I6","question":"Does the clinician counsel the patient on hereditary transmission risk, family screening implications, or the role of genetic counseling?","include_when":"The condition has a recognized familial or genetic component, or the patient asks about inheritance risk for relatives.","exclude_when":"No hereditary condition or patient question regarding familial risk is present.","adequate":"Clinician accurately explains the relevant inheritance pattern or low familial risk and discusses the value of family notification or genetic counseling (while test ordering and referral execution are handled by MOD-02 and MOD-08).","insufficient":"Clinician ignores family-risk questions or provides inaccurate heritability reassurance.","basis":"unresolved_source_support"}]},"specialist_04":{"id":"specialist_04","title":"Pharmacotherapy & Safety Management","subtitle":"MOD-04 · 7 assigned screening topics","kind":"model","plain":"This specialist checks treatment choices, contraindications, interactions and medication safety. It selects topics that matter for this case and turns them into specific evaluation criteria. Topics that do not apply, or remain uncertain, need reasons.","technical":"Initiating or continuing indicated therapy; resolving allergy, comorbidity, drug-interaction, and organ-function contraindications; stopping exacerbating agents; mitigating reproductive/teratogenic risk; stewarding antimicrobials and safe OTC use; and executing sequencing, titration, step-down, or adverse reaction adjustments. Exact catalog questions below are candidate element types, not automatic scored criteria. All eight collectively screen 50 types. Keep applicability, inclusion rationale, excluded proposals, overlap ownership and unresolved facets.","input":"The same eligible chart, source-bound authorized scenario, task and corrected minimums; this specialist’s assigned checklist module; optional current guidance.","output":"Case-specific proposals, source rationale, notes and applicability decisions for the integrator.","status":"Implemented locally and checked with invented fixtures. The default Sol authoring/grading route is blocked by provider credits; no live default Sol rubric or grade has been produced. Human rubric review remains pending.","boundary":"Generic topics can describe acts outside this encounter’s capabilities. Adapt only to the declared task; do not add dummy actor criteria, require unavailable actions, score the patient or stand-in, or treat template examples as clinical approval. Test-purpose.minimum.v2 remains a distinct pre-authoring correction. The retained supplied minimum owns its protected requirement; use minimum_ids for matching checklist coverage instead of a duplicate criterion.","topics":[{"id":"M4-I1","question":"Does the clinician initiate or continue indicated condition-directed or supportive pharmacotherapy?","include_when":"Clinical findings or confirmed diagnoses warrant starting first-line disease-directed or symptomatic treatment, or maintaining an effective established regimen.","exclude_when":"Management is exclusively non-pharmacological, or pharmacotherapy must be deferred pending diagnostic confirmation or emergency transfer.","adequate":"Clinician prescribes or continues an appropriate, case-supported therapeutic class for the condition (with clinically sound alternatives accepted).","insufficient":"Clinician omits indicated first-line treatment or prematurely discontinues necessary maintenance therapy.","basis":"workbook_exemplar"},{"id":"M4-I2","question":"Does the clinician select or switch pharmacotherapy to avoid a drug-disease, drug-drug, organ-function, or allergy contraindication?","include_when":"Standard therapy conflicts with a documented allergy, comorbidity, concurrent medication, or renal/hepatic impairment.","exclude_when":"First-line standard therapy has no contraindications or interactions in the patient.","adequate":"Clinician recognizes the constraint, avoids or stops the contraindicated agent, and selects a safe alternative therapeutic class.","insufficient":"Clinician prescribes or continues a contraindicated agent or stops needed treatment without offering a safe alternative.","basis":"unresolved_source_support"},{"id":"M4-I3","question":"Does the clinician discontinue or substitute an over-the-counter or prescribed agent that is actively causing or exacerbating the clinical problem?","include_when":"An active prescribed, over-the-counter, or borrowed medication is driving pathology, symptom flares, or vital sign instability.","exclude_when":"No current medication is exacerbating the presenting condition.","adequate":"Clinician explains the iatrogenic link, advises stopping and avoiding the offending agent, and provides a safer option for symptom relief if needed.","insufficient":"Clinician treats the resulting symptoms while allowing the offending agent to continue.","basis":"unresolved_source_support"},{"id":"M4-I4","question":"Does the clinician address reproductive potential, preconception washout/contraception, or pregnancy safety when prescribing or reviewing therapy?","include_when":"A patient of childbearing potential is prescribed or taking a teratogenic medication, planning conception on high-risk therapy, or currently pregnant.","exclude_when":"Reproductive, preconception, or antenatal medication safety considerations do not apply.","adequate":"Clinician counsels on fetal/teratogenic risks, coordinates effective contraception or safe preconception medication transition/washout, avoids teratogens in pregnancy, and recommends indicated prenatal prophylaxis.","insufficient":"Clinician initiates or continues teratogenic therapy without contraception/washout counseling or leaves unsafe medications unaddressed in pregnancy.","basis":"unresolved_source_support"},{"id":"M4-I5","question":"Does the clinician make an appropriate antimicrobial prescribing or withholding decision and align antimicrobial selection with clinical or microbiologic indications?","include_when":"The current encounter involves a suspected viral or bacterial infection requiring an antimicrobial prescribing, withholding, or culture-directed adjustment decision.","exclude_when":"No infectious presentation or antimicrobial therapy decision applies in the current encounter (symptomatic OTC medication safety boundaries are screened separately under M4-I7).","adequate":"Clinician withholds unneeded antibiotics in self-limiting viral illness with an actionable contingency plan, or selects/adjusts antimicrobial therapy to match clinical and culture susceptibility indications.","insufficient":"Clinician prescribes unwarranted immediate antibiotics for a likely viral illness or continues/initiates an antimicrobial agent discordant with known susceptibilities or clinical indication.","basis":"unresolved_source_support"},{"id":"M4-I6","question":"Does the clinician execute appropriate physiological sequencing, dose titration, step-down/deprescribing, or adverse drug reaction adjustment?","include_when":"Therapy requires staged initiation after confirmation, physiological sequencing, escalation for suboptimal control, deprescribing after remission, or modification for a treatment-emergent adverse effect.","exclude_when":"Unchanged maintenance therapy is effective and well tolerated.","adequate":"Clinician sequences interventions safely, titrates or adds therapy when control is inadequate, steps down therapy upon sustained remission with relapse contingencies, or stops/adjusts regimens causing adverse effects.","insufficient":"Clinician reverses safe physiological sequencing, leaves suboptimal control unaddressed, continues short-term therapy indefinitely without review, or ignores treatment-emergent adverse reactions.","basis":"unresolved_source_support"},{"id":"M4-I7","question":"Does the clinician counsel on safe usage boundaries, dosing limits, or overuse risks for symptomatic over-the-counter medications?","include_when":"The current encounter involves use, patient request, or clinician recommendation of symptomatic over-the-counter analgesics, antipyretics, or decongestants carrying toxicity, rebound, or medication-overuse risks.","exclude_when":"No symptomatic over-the-counter medication safety or overuse boundary applies in the current encounter (antimicrobial decisions are screened under M4-I5).","adequate":"Clinician advises safe dosing frequency, maximum duration limits, and explicit precautions to prevent medication-overuse headache, rebound congestion, or organ toxicity from symptomatic OTC agents.","insufficient":"Clinician recommends or endorses symptomatic OTC medications carrying rebound or overuse risks without specifying safe duration or dosing boundaries.","basis":"unresolved_source_support"}]},"specialist_05":{"id":"specialist_05","title":"Shared Decision-Making & Communication Completeness","subtitle":"MOD-05 · 6 assigned screening topics","kind":"model","plain":"This specialist checks patient concerns, preferences, choices and complete communication. It selects topics that matter for this case and turns them into specific evaluation criteria. Topics that do not apply, or remain uncertain, need reasons.","technical":"Responding to patient concerns, questions, and delivery preferences; weighing individualized risk-benefit trade-offs and assessing decision-making capacity; counseling on conservative-to-surgical hierarchies; addressing unproven remedies and funding boundaries; negotiating care refusal with actionable deferral; and delivering complete communication across turns. Exact catalog questions below are candidate element types, not automatic scored criteria. All eight collectively screen 50 types. Keep applicability, inclusion rationale, excluded proposals, overlap ownership and unresolved facets.","input":"The same eligible chart, source-bound authorized scenario, task and corrected minimums; this specialist’s assigned checklist module; optional current guidance.","output":"Case-specific proposals, source rationale, notes and applicability decisions for the integrator.","status":"Implemented locally and checked with invented fixtures. The default Sol authoring/grading route is blocked by provider credits; no live default Sol rubric or grade has been produced. Human rubric review remains pending.","boundary":"Generic topics can describe acts outside this encounter’s capabilities. Adapt only to the declared task; do not add dummy actor criteria, require unavailable actions, score the patient or stand-in, or treat template examples as clinical approval. Test-purpose.minimum.v2 remains a distinct pre-authoring correction. The retained supplied minimum owns its protected requirement; use minimum_ids for matching checklist coverage instead of a duplicate criterion.","topics":[{"id":"M5-I1","question":"Does the clinician explore and respond to the patient's specific concerns, scripted questions, treatment reluctance, or delivery-route preferences?","include_when":"The patient or caregiver raises questions or concerns, expresses hesitation about starting therapy, or prefers a specific administration route or device.","exclude_when":"No specific patient question, concern, reluctance, or route preference is present.","adequate":"Clinician directly answers the patient's questions, validates concerns about side effects or long-term use, and accommodates safe route or device preferences (or explains clearly when a preferred option is clinically unsafe).","insufficient":"Clinician ignores scripted patient questions, dismisses reluctance, or overrides safe patient preferences without explanation.","basis":"workbook_exemplar"},{"id":"M5-I2","question":"Does the clinician guide shared decision-making across competing clinical risks and benefits, including assessing decision-making capacity and consent when insight is impaired?","include_when":"Initiating, extending, or stopping high-stakes therapy involves major competing risks (such as thrombosis vs. bleeding, or neuropsychiatric medication risk in frailty/dementia) or impaired patient insight.","exclude_when":"Treatment follows a straightforward standard without major competing risk trade-offs or capacity concerns.","adequate":"Clinician clearly explains the competing clinical risks and benefits, incorporates patient/family values, and assesses decision-making capacity and informed consent when cognitive impairment or anosognosia is present.","insufficient":"Clinician applies a rigid rule without discussing competing risks or initiates high-risk therapy in an impaired patient without addressing capacity and consent.","basis":"unresolved_source_support"},{"id":"M5-I3","question":"Does the clinician counsel on the hierarchy of conservative, device, minimally invasive, and surgical options?","include_when":"The condition has staged non-surgical and procedural/surgical pathways, or requires explaining why definitive procedural intervention is indicated.","exclude_when":"Management is purely medical and no procedural or surgical options are relevant or raised.","adequate":"Clinician outlines the appropriate progression from conservative/behavioral measures to device or surgical interventions, clarifies procedural expectations and risks, and explains when surgery is optional versus definitively indicated.","insufficient":"Clinician escalates directly to invasive procedures when conservative care is first-line, or omits procedural option counseling.","basis":"workbook_exemplar"},{"id":"M5-I4","question":"Does the clinician provide evidence-based counseling when asked about unproven remedies, supplements, or healthcare funding/eligibility boundaries?","include_when":"The patient asks about complementary supplements, non-recommended modalities, or publicly funded procedures for cosmetic/asymptomatic indications.","exclude_when":"The patient does not inquire about unproven remedies or coverage boundaries.","adequate":"Clinician respectfully explains the lack of efficacy evidence or condition-specific drawbacks and clarifies clinical eligibility versus private/cosmetic options where applicable.","insufficient":"Clinician endorses ineffective interventions as evidence-based or dismisses the patient's inquiry without explanation.","basis":"unresolved_source_support"},{"id":"M5-I5","question":"When a patient hesitates or declines a recommended test or treatment, does the clinician negotiate respectfully and establish an actionable deferral or interim bridging plan?","include_when":"A patient delays, hesitates, or declines recommended diagnostic testing, medication, device therapy, or surgery.","exclude_when":"The patient readily accepts the recommended diagnostic and therapeutic plan.","adequate":"Clinician explores the underlying barrier, explains the clinical rationale without coercion, and arranges an actionable interim safety, bridging, or reassessment plan (scored solely on clinician counseling, never on patient consent).","insufficient":"Clinician pressures the patient, fails to explain the rationale for recommended care, or abandons interim safety planning when the patient declines.","basis":"design_extension"},{"id":"M5-I6","question":"Does the clinician deliver complete, coherent explanations across dialogue turns rather than making unfulfilled promises to explain later?","include_when":"The encounter requires multi-step diagnostic, therapeutic, or prognostic counseling across interactive turns.","exclude_when":"No substantive clinical explanation or counseling is required at the visit.","adequate":"Clinician completes required explanations clearly and coherently across the conversation, ensuring that any topic introduced or promised is substantively delivered before the encounter ends.","insufficient":"Clinician substitutes vague promises (e.g., 'I will explain that later' or 'read the leaflet') for required verbal counseling, or leaves key explanations truncated across turns.","basis":"design_extension"}]},"specialist_06":{"id":"specialist_06","title":"Treatment Instruction & Comprehension Verification","subtitle":"MOD-06 · 6 assigned screening topics","kind":"model","plain":"This specialist checks practical treatment instructions, adherence and whether understanding is verified. It selects topics that matter for this case and turns them into specific evaluation criteria. Topics that do not apply, or remain uncertain, need reasons.","technical":"Actual patient teaching on medical device technique, medication administration mechanics, therapeutic onset/adherence/maintenance-vs-rescue roles, structured self-monitoring logs, pre-escalation adherence/technique verification, and meaningful comprehension checks. Exact catalog questions below are candidate element types, not automatic scored criteria. All eight collectively screen 50 types. Keep applicability, inclusion rationale, excluded proposals, overlap ownership and unresolved facets.","input":"The same eligible chart, source-bound authorized scenario, task and corrected minimums; this specialist’s assigned checklist module; optional current guidance.","output":"Case-specific proposals, source rationale, notes and applicability decisions for the integrator.","status":"Implemented locally and checked with invented fixtures. The default Sol authoring/grading route is blocked by provider credits; no live default Sol rubric or grade has been produced. Human rubric review remains pending.","boundary":"Generic topics can describe acts outside this encounter’s capabilities. Adapt only to the declared task; do not add dummy actor criteria, require unavailable actions, score the patient or stand-in, or treat template examples as clinical approval. Test-purpose.minimum.v2 remains a distinct pre-authoring correction. The retained supplied minimum owns its protected requirement; use minimum_ids for matching checklist coverage instead of a duplicate criterion.","topics":[{"id":"M6-I1","question":"Does the clinician actually teach or troubleshoot medical device technique rather than merely prescribing or promising instruction?","include_when":"The management plan initiates, continues, or adjusts an inhaler, spacer, peak flow meter, positive airway pressure device, pessary, or compression garment.","exclude_when":"No medical device or specialized delivery apparatus is involved.","adequate":"Clinician actively explains step-by-step device use, reviews technique, or troubleshoots fit/tolerance issues during the visit.","insufficient":"Clinician prescribes or escalates a device without explaining how to use it, or merely promises that someone else will demonstrate it later.","basis":"unresolved_source_support"},{"id":"M6-I2","question":"Does the clinician instruct the patient on required medication timing, meal/protein coordination, posture, or supplement separation?","include_when":"Prescribed therapy requires specific administration mechanics to ensure absorption, prevent mucosal injury, or avoid diurnal side effects.","exclude_when":"Prescribed medications have no special timing, food, postural, or separation requirements.","adequate":"Clinician gives explicit, plain-language instructions on when and how to take the medication relative to meals, sleep, posture, or other supplements.","insufficient":"Clinician prescribes a timing- or posture-sensitive medication without administration instructions.","basis":"unresolved_source_support"},{"id":"M6-I3","question":"Does the clinician explain medication mechanism, expected time to onset, full-course or long-term adherence, and maintenance-versus-rescue roles?","include_when":"The patient starts antimicrobials, preventive/prophylactic agents with delayed onset, chronic disease controllers, or dual maintenance and reliever therapy.","exclude_when":"No pharmacotherapy requiring onset, course-completion, or controller-versus-reliever education is active.","adequate":"Clinician explains what the medication does, when to expect clinical benefit, why completing a course or continuing chronic therapy matters after symptoms improve, and how to distinguish daily maintenance from rescue use.","insufficient":"Clinician issues a prescription without explaining delayed onset, course completion, or controller versus reliever roles.","basis":"unresolved_source_support"},{"id":"M6-I4","question":"Does the clinician instruct the patient on maintaining a structured self-monitoring log and explain actionable target ranges?","include_when":"Longitudinal diagnosis or titration depends on home physiological measurements or structured symptom/trigger diaries.","exclude_when":"Structured home physiological or symptom logging is not clinically relevant.","adequate":"Clinician specifies what parameters to record, measurement frequency, and what target ranges or patterns should prompt review.","insufficient":"Clinician advises vague self-observation without defining structured logging parameters or target expectations.","basis":"workbook_exemplar"},{"id":"M6-I5","question":"Before escalating dose or switching therapy for suboptimal control, does the clinician verify medication adherence, dietary implementation, and device technique?","include_when":"A follow-up visit shows incomplete symptom or biomarker control on an initial therapeutic regimen.","exclude_when":"Initial therapy is effective, or the encounter is an initial baseline assessment.","adequate":"Clinician explicitly checks how consistently the patient is taking the medication, following dietary measures, and using any delivery device before up-titrating or switching classes.","insufficient":"Clinician escalates doses or adds second-line agents without checking adherence or administration technique.","basis":"design_extension"},{"id":"M6-I6","question":"After delivering complex teaching or action plans, does the clinician perform a meaningful understanding check and address low, non-committal, or absent patient responses?","include_when":"The clinician delivers device instruction, multi-step medication/prep rules, or complex safety-netting during the encounter.","exclude_when":"No complex instruction was required, OR the prerequisite teaching/explanation was omitted by the clinician (to prevent duplicate dependent penalties).","adequate":"Clinician invites the patient or caregiver to summarize or demonstrate how they will carry out the plan, and if the response is minimal, confused, or absent, clarifies and re-engages supportively (scored strictly on clinician behavior).","insufficient":"Clinician finishes complex instruction without checking comprehension, or ignores cues of confusion or minimal/non-committal responses.","basis":"design_extension"}]},"specialist_07":{"id":"specialist_07","title":"Functional Adaptation, Public/Legal Safety & Prevention","subtitle":"MOD-07 · 7 assigned screening topics","kind":"model","plain":"This specialist checks daily-life adaptations, safety, prevention and caregiver support. It selects topics that matter for this case and turns them into specific evaluation criteria. Topics that do not apply, or remain uncertain, need reasons.","technical":"Condition-specific non-pharmacological self-care and nutrition/bone health adapted to daily life; occupational hazard mitigation and graded return to work; driving, household, wandering, and advance care safety planning; substance cessation support; infection transmission precautions; and preventive immunizations and psychosocial/caregiver coaching. Exact catalog questions below are candidate element types, not automatic scored criteria. All eight collectively screen 50 types. Keep applicability, inclusion rationale, excluded proposals, overlap ownership and unresolved facets.","input":"The same eligible chart, source-bound authorized scenario, task and corrected minimums; this specialist’s assigned checklist module; optional current guidance.","output":"Case-specific proposals, source rationale, notes and applicability decisions for the integrator.","status":"Implemented locally and checked with invented fixtures. The default Sol authoring/grading route is blocked by provider credits; no live default Sol rubric or grade has been produced. Human rubric review remains pending.","boundary":"Generic topics can describe acts outside this encounter’s capabilities. Adapt only to the declared task; do not add dummy actor criteria, require unavailable actions, score the patient or stand-in, or treat template examples as clinical approval. Test-purpose.minimum.v2 remains a distinct pre-authoring correction. The retained supplied minimum owns its protected requirement; use minimum_ids for matching checklist coverage instead of a duplicate criterion.","topics":[{"id":"M7-I1","question":"Does the clinician provide condition-specific non-pharmacological self-care, dietary, bone-health, or activity strategies tailored to the patient's daily constraints?","include_when":"Targeted dietary, postural, fluid, bone-health, physical activity, or symptom-management measures directly modify disease activity or symptom burden.","exclude_when":"Non-pharmacological lifestyle measures have no specific therapeutic role in the visit.","adequate":"Clinician recommends actionable, condition-specific dietary, bone-protective, physical, or self-care adaptations tailored to the patient's routine and disclosed barriers.","insufficient":"Clinician offers only generic wellness platitudes or ignores practical barriers in the patient's daily schedule.","basis":"workbook_exemplar"},{"id":"M7-I2","question":"Does the clinician advise on occupational hazard mitigation, workplace ergonomic adjustments, or graded return-to-work and activity restrictions?","include_when":"Workplace exposures or physical demands aggravate the condition, or recovery/cardiopulmonary risk requires temporary activity restriction or graded reintegration.","exclude_when":"Occupation and physical activities are unaffected by the condition or treatment.","adequate":"Clinician provides concrete workplace exposure/ergonomic modifications, activity restrictions pending clearance, or a graded return-to-work plan.","insufficient":"Clinician overlooks occupational triggers or fails to guide safe activity restriction and work reintegration.","basis":"unresolved_source_support"},{"id":"M7-I3","question":"Does the clinician counsel on driving/machinery safety, household/fall/wandering/seizure precautions, or advance care planning when capacity or consciousness is affected?","include_when":"The presentation involves syncope, seizures, daytime somnolence, visual/motor impairment, or progressive cognitive decline affecting driving, home safety, or future decision-making capacity.","exclude_when":"Consciousness, motor/visual control, and cognitive capacity for driving and independent safety are intact.","adequate":"Clinician advises appropriate driving precautions and regulatory notification per case context, addresses household/seizure/wandering safety, and discusses future care/legal planning in progressive cognitive decline.","insufficient":"Clinician omits driving or home-safety precautions in high-risk neurological/cardiopulmonary states or neglects future planning in progressive dementia.","basis":"unresolved_source_support"},{"id":"M7-I4","question":"Does the clinician link active tobacco, alcohol, or substance use to the patient's condition and offer cessation or harm-reduction support?","include_when":"Active smoking, excess alcohol, or substance use drives disease progression, surgical/bleeding risk, medication toxicity, or transplant eligibility.","exclude_when":"The patient does not use tobacco, excess alcohol, or harmful substances.","adequate":"Clinician explains how the substance directly affects the patient's condition or treatment safety and offers nonjudgmental behavioral or pharmacological cessation support.","insufficient":"Clinician notes substance use in history but fails to explain its clinical impact or offer cessation support.","basis":"workbook_exemplar"},{"id":"M7-I5","question":"Does the clinician counsel on infection transmission precautions, hygiene measures, or contact tracing when a transmissible condition is present?","include_when":"The patient has a communicable respiratory, enteric, bloodborne, or sexually transmitted infection, or recurrent infection linked to hygiene practices.","exclude_when":"No communicable infection or hygiene-preventable recurrence risk is present in the current encounter.","adequate":"Clinician instructs the patient on isolation, masking, barrier protection, hygiene, or household/partner contact tracing matched to the transmission route.","insufficient":"Clinician treats the infection pharmacotherapeutically but omits indicated transmission precautions, hygiene measures, or contact tracing counseling.","basis":"unresolved_source_support"},{"id":"M7-I6","question":"Does the clinician recommend indicated preventive immunizations matched to the patient's condition, age, or exposure risk?","include_when":"Chronic disease, age, clinical status, or exposure risk in the current encounter warrants preventive vaccination counseling.","exclude_when":"No preventive immunization indication is present in the current encounter (in-encounter psychosocial or caregiver support is screened separately under M7-I7).","adequate":"Clinician recommends relevant preventive vaccinations and explains their role in preventing avoidable complications for the patient's risk profile.","insufficient":"Clinician omits indicated preventive immunization advice when current-encounter facts establish a clear vaccination need.","basis":"unresolved_source_support"},{"id":"M7-I7","question":"Does the clinician provide empathetic psychosocial validation and practical in-encounter behavioral or caregiver coping support?","include_when":"The patient or caregiver experiences illness-related emotional distress, bereavement, behavioral challenges, or caregiving strain during the current encounter.","exclude_when":"No psychosocial distress or caregiver strain is present in the current encounter (preventive immunization is screened under M7-I6 and formal service referrals belong to MOD-08 M8-I3).","adequate":"Clinician acknowledges emotional or caregiver burden empathetically and offers practical in-encounter behavioral coping or redirection strategies (while formal service referrals belong to MOD-08).","insufficient":"Clinician ignores overt patient distress or caregiver exhaustion, or offers dismissive reassurance without practical in-encounter supportive guidance.","basis":"unresolved_source_support"}]},"specialist_08":{"id":"specialist_08","title":"Care Coordination, Surveillance & Follow-Up Closure","subtitle":"MOD-08 · 6 assigned screening topics","kind":"model","plain":"This specialist checks urgent escalation, referrals, monitoring, safety-netting and follow-up. It selects topics that matter for this case and turns them into specific evaluation criteria. Topics that do not apply, or remain uncertain, need reasons.","technical":"Immediate emergency/inpatient transfer triage; medical, surgical, obstetric, and urgent cancer specialist referrals; allied health, rehabilitation, dietetics, optometry, and social support referrals; longitudinal lab/imaging surveillance schedules; condition-specific red-flag safety-netting; and routine follow-up scheduling with pending-result loop closure. Exact catalog questions below are candidate element types, not automatic scored criteria. All eight collectively screen 50 types. Keep applicability, inclusion rationale, excluded proposals, overlap ownership and unresolved facets.","input":"The same eligible chart, source-bound authorized scenario, task and corrected minimums; this specialist’s assigned checklist module; optional current guidance.","output":"Case-specific proposals, source rationale, notes and applicability decisions for the integrator.","status":"Implemented locally and checked with invented fixtures. The default Sol authoring/grading route is blocked by provider credits; no live default Sol rubric or grade has been produced. Human rubric review remains pending.","boundary":"Generic topics can describe acts outside this encounter’s capabilities. Adapt only to the declared task; do not add dummy actor criteria, require unavailable actions, score the patient or stand-in, or treat template examples as clinical approval. Test-purpose.minimum.v2 remains a distinct pre-authoring correction. The retained supplied minimum owns its protected requirement; use minimum_ids for matching checklist coverage instead of a duplicate criterion.","topics":[{"id":"M8-I1","question":"Does the clinician arrange immediate emergency department transfer or hospital admission when the presentation is acutely unstable?","include_when":"The patient exhibits life- or organ-threatening instability requiring immediate inpatient resuscitation, monitoring, or emergency intervention.","exclude_when":"The patient is clinically stable for outpatient evaluation and management.","adequate":"Clinician recognizes the acute instability, explains why outpatient care is unsafe, and arranges immediate emergency transfer or hospital admission.","insufficient":"Clinician manages an unstable emergency with routine outpatient testing or delayed clinic follow-up.","basis":"unresolved_source_support"},{"id":"M8-I2","question":"Does the clinician initiate indicated medical, surgical, obstetric, or urgent suspected-cancer specialist referrals and coordinate cross-specialty priorities?","include_when":"Definitive diagnosis, procedural intervention, high-risk pregnancy/comorbidity co-management, or suspected malignancy requires specialist referral.","exclude_when":"The condition is appropriately managed within primary care without specialist referral.","adequate":"Clinician initiates the appropriate specialist or multidisciplinary referral at the warranted urgency tier and coordinates perioperative or cross-specialty priorities.","insufficient":"Clinician omits a needed specialist referral, refers prematurely when primary care management is indicated, or fails to coordinate conflicting specialty needs.","basis":"unresolved_source_support"},{"id":"M8-I3","question":"Does the clinician coordinate indicated allied health, structured rehabilitation, dietetics, optometry/ophthalmology, or social support referrals?","include_when":"Multidisciplinary non-physician services (such as cardiopulmonary rehab, physiotherapy, occupational therapy home assessment, dietitian, retinal/eye screening, psychotherapy, or social/respite services) are clinically supported.","exclude_when":"No allied health, rehabilitation, eye-care, or social support referrals are indicated.","adequate":"Clinician recommends and coordinates the specific multidisciplinary referrals matched to the patient's functional, nutritional, visual, or psychosocial needs.","insufficient":"Clinician relies solely on brief verbal advice when structured rehabilitation, dietetics, eye screening, or social care referral is warranted.","basis":"workbook_exemplar"},{"id":"M8-I4","question":"Does the clinician establish a scheduled longitudinal laboratory, biomarker, imaging, or endoscopic surveillance plan?","include_when":"Ongoing management requires periodic objective monitoring for treatment efficacy, drug toxicity, post-procedural recurrence, or silent disease progression.","exclude_when":"The condition is acute and self-limiting with no requirement for longitudinal biochemical or imaging surveillance.","adequate":"Clinician outlines a condition-appropriate schedule for repeat laboratory, drug-safety, imaging, or endoscopic monitoring and explains its purpose.","insufficient":"Clinician starts chronic/high-risk therapy or diagnoses a condition requiring surveillance without arranging longitudinal monitoring.","basis":"unresolved_source_support"},{"id":"M8-I5","question":"Does the clinician provide condition-specific red-flag warning signs, non-improvement thresholds, and emergency escalation instructions?","include_when":"The working diagnosis or outpatient treatment carries a specific risk of acute deterioration, complication, or treatment failure.","exclude_when":"The patient is being transferred immediately to emergency/inpatient care at the current visit, or no plausible acute deterioration pathway applies.","adequate":"Clinician names specific warning symptoms or non-improvement signs relevant to the condition/treatment and states clearly when to seek urgent clinic review versus immediate emergency care.","insufficient":"Clinician gives only generic 'return if worse' advice without specifying warning signs or escalation urgency.","basis":"unresolved_source_support"},{"id":"M8-I6","question":"Does the clinician schedule an appropriate routine follow-up interval and modality and close the loop on any pending investigation results?","include_when":"The visit requires a scheduled review appointment, transition to as-needed follow-up upon remission, or a plan to review newly ordered or still-pending test results.","exclude_when":"The patient is transferred immediately to inpatient care and no outpatient follow-up or pending-result plan is applicable at this stage.","adequate":"Clinician specifies the timeframe and modality (in-person vs. virtual) for the next review (or PRN discharge upon resolution), states what will be reassessed, and explains how and when pending test results will be communicated.","insufficient":"Clinician orders tests or adjusts therapy without arranging follow-up or explaining how pending results will be reviewed.","basis":"workbook_exemplar"}]}},"views":[{"id":"overview","title":"The integrated pipeline: from chart to reviewable case","short":"Overview","subtitle":"One patient · two conversations · asynchronous review","description":"Follow the main flow below, then use the six detailed views to inspect each sub-pipeline. Simulation proceeds independently of rubric authoring and human review; provisional grading starts when both a valid frozen rubric and saved evidence are available. All artifacts, including partial conversations and failures, are saved as they become available; storage does not wait for a final plan or grade. Case-wide display rule: this reviewer must judge all required case runs before any method label or AI grade is revealed.","groups":[["stages","Reading order",["eligible","prepareOverview","author","sp","patient","intake","summary","standin","finalap","specialists","rubric","assess","dossier","initial","reveal","report"]]],"edges":[["eligible","prepareOverview","eligible chart"],["eligible","author","eligible chart + authorized scenario"],["prepareOverview","intake","prepared chart brief"],["author","patient","same selected packet"],["author","sp","same selected packet"],["patient","intake","same patient: first conversation"],["patient","standin","same patient: second conversation"],["intake","summary","intake transcript + alerts"],["summary","standin","case data: handoff only"],["eligible","finalap","eligible original chart"],["summary","finalap","co-clinician handoff"],["intake","finalap","saved intake transcript"],["standin","finalap","saved clinician transcript"],["eligible","specialists","case facts only"],["specialists","rubric","team flow: see view 06"],["rubric","assess","same frozen version per case"],["prepareOverview","assess","accepted brief + omissions"],["intake","assess","intake transcript"],["summary","assess","handoff + alerts"],["standin","assess","encounter evidence, not scored"],["finalap","assess","final plan"],["finalap","dossier","final plan, if reached"],["assess","dossier","stored provisional feedback"],["dossier","initial","all case runs; method/grades hidden"],["initial","reveal","this reviewer judged every case run"],["dossier","reveal","method, rubric, available grades"],["dossier","report","same facts; unblinded report"],["prepareOverview","summary","prepared chart context"],["eligible","assess","eligible source evidence"],["rubric","dossier","rubric versions, as available","dashed"],["intake","patient","heard speech kept for second talk","dashed"],["initial","dossier","separate judgments for every run","dashed"]],"boundary":"Human review does not delay simulation, grading or storage","note":"The end products are a readable case dossier, human SP documents, provisional three-area feedback and asynchronous human review. The same patient takes part twice. No clinical approval is implied. Rubric team and Review & report show finalized human versions and pending regrades in both methods, with original feedback retained. Judgments remain per run; reveal permission is case-wide and reviewer-specific. Runtime enforcement of this newly adopted requirement is still pending."},{"id":"preparation","title":"Two ways to prepare the chart","short":"Chart preparation","subtitle":"Direct writing or extraction plus code assembly","description":"Repeat chart preparation for each run. Patient authoring is separate and shared; neither preparation method is assumed better.","groups":[["stages","Reading order",["chart","eligible","route","direct","extract","assemble","check","handoff","failure"]]],"edges":[["chart","eligible","chart inventory"],["eligible","route","eligible records + task"],["route","direct","eligible records: direct method"],["route","extract","eligible records: extraction method"],["extract","assemble","accepted statements + exact references"],["eligible","assemble","existing structured records","dashed"],["direct","check","draft brief"],["assemble","check","assembled brief"],["eligible","check","original sources for checks","dashed"],["check","handoff","accepted brief + references + explicit omissions"],["extract","failure","invalid / failed / unknown","dashed"],["check","failure","blocked output; dependents stop","dashed"],["direct","failure","invalid / failed / unknown","dashed"],["assemble","failure","invalid assembly; dependents stop","dashed"]],"boundary":"Both methods have the same prepared-brief grading target","note":"The accepted brief, references and omissions are graded as chart-preparation fidelity (schema ID chart_extraction). Quote checks establish traceability, not that every source supports a claim or that the brief is clinically complete."},{"id":"patientview","title":"One selected patient packet for simulation and people","short":"Patient & SP","subtitle":"Traceable knowledge, portrayal and continuity","description":"The same packet drives the simulated patient and human SP handouts. A separate addendum carries actual intake speech into the second conversation.","groups":[["stages","Reading order",["eligible","scenario","author","memory","caseReview","v18","sp","patient","intake","transcript","continuity","standin"]]],"edges":[["eligible","author","eligible chart evidence for authoring"],["scenario","author","shared case scenario"],["author","memory","version-bound patient packet"],["memory","caseReview","optional asynchronous inspection","dashed"],["memory","v18","same selected version"],["memory","sp","same selected version"],["v18","patient","simulation only"],["sp","patient","human practice alternative","dashed"],["patient","intake","first conversation"],["intake","transcript","both speakers: actual intake speech"],["transcript","continuity","what patient heard and said"],["continuity","patient","for second conversation; separate addendum"],["continuity","sp","optional addendum for human practice","dashed"],["patient","standin","same patient in second conversation"],["caseReview","memory","new version for future runs; frozen runs unchanged","dashed"]],"boundary":"Patient knowledge is narrower than the chart","note":"No handoff, hidden chart, internal plan or rubric goes to the patient. Unknown source settings stay unknown; labeled moderate defaults are simulation assumptions. Human review is optional/versioned here, not a new run gate. Patient performance is not scored by the clinician rubric."},{"id":"intake","title":"Co-clinician intake and its distinct handoff","short":"Intake","subtitle":"Patient agenda, chart checks and alerts","description":"Use the prepared chart brief to gather relevant updates. Compare chart and patient reports, then hand over a tentative assessment and plan.","groups":[["stages","Reading order",["handoff","v18","patient","intake","references","transcript","urgent","summary","handoffDestinations"]]],"edges":[["handoff","intake","prepared chart brief"],["v18","patient","simulation prompt only"],["intake","patient","questions / discussion"],["patient","intake","actual answers"],["intake","references","approved nonidentifying query","dashed"],["references","intake","optional dated guidance","dashed"],["intake","transcript","co-clinician speech"],["patient","transcript","patient speech"],["intake","urgent","model-reported concern"],["urgent","summary","code always inserts recorded alerts"],["handoff","summary","chart context for discrepancies"],["transcript","summary","completed intake evidence"],["summary","handoffDestinations","co-clinician handoff"],["transcript","handoffDestinations","transcript to final-plan writer/grader; not stand-in"]],"boundary":"Alerts stay visible while intake can continue","note":"The host records a soft-limit event at 16 co-clinician turns per conversation and stops at 24 without a quota prompt. A stopped run keeps its partial transcript and stops dependent stages. Intake plus handoff/alerts are graded later; the stand-in is not. Alerts and reference-tool logs remain saved in adjacent run records even if intake stops; only actual speech belongs in the transcript."},{"id":"clinical","title":"Second conversation and draft assessment / plan","short":"Encounter & A/P","subtitle":"A distinct clinician conversation with the same patient","description":"The stand-in receives the co-clinician handoff as its only case-data input. The final-plan writer sees eligible chart, handoff and both saved transcripts. All artifacts, including partial conversations and failures, are saved as they become available; storage does not wait for a final plan or grade.","groups":[["stages","Reading order",["summary","continuity","patient","standin","transcript","encounterRecord","eligible","references","finalap","dossier"]]],"edges":[["summary","standin","case data: handoff only"],["continuity","patient","patient-heard intake speech"],["standin","patient","clinician discussion"],["patient","standin","patient answers"],["standin","encounterRecord","clinician turns"],["patient","encounterRecord","patient turns"],["transcript","finalap","saved intake conversation (see Intake view)"],["encounterRecord","finalap","completed second-conversation record"],["summary","finalap","co-clinician handoff"],["eligible","finalap","eligible original chart"],["references","standin","optional; off by default","dashed"],["references","finalap","optional current guidance","dashed"],["finalap","dossier","preserved draft and evidence"],["transcript","continuity","actual spoken turns, including suggestions"],["encounterRecord","dossier","complete or partial encounter record"]],"boundary":"Discussion, patient response and later considerations remain distinct","note":"No examination, executed order/treatment, final diagnosis or approval is implied. A stopped second conversation does not generate a completed final plan. Final A/P is a graded output; encounter-attributable gaps remain separate from final-writer errors."},{"id":"rubrics","title":"A shared rubric from specialist proposals to grading","short":"Rubric team","subtitle":"Eight specialists collectively screen 50 types","description":"Fresh authors use the same source-bound case/scenario and assigned topic modules. Model decisions and fixed-code assembly steps are separate steps. Press Eight rubric-writing specialists to expand their individual boxes; press a specialist for its assigned screening topics. Saved run evidence reaches the grader through the Review & report view.","groups":[["stages","Reading order",["eligible","scenario","checklist","floor","minimum","references","specialists","integrator","preliminary","peers","modelReconcile","finalAssembly","rubric","freeze","assess","semantic","revise"]]],"edges":[["eligible","specialists","same eligible chart"],["scenario","specialists","authorized scenario + source passages"],["checklist","specialists","six or seven assigned types each"],["floor","minimum","declared intake requirements"],["minimum","specialists","corrected before authoring"],["references","specialists","optional current guidance","dashed"],["references","peers","optional current guidance","dashed"],["specialists","integrator","eight preserved proposal sets"],["integrator","preliminary","decisions by proposal ID"],["preliminary","peers","assembled preliminary rubric"],["peers","modelReconcile","eight retained critiques"],["modelReconcile","finalAssembly","decisions for each issue"],["finalAssembly","rubric","only if structurally valid"],["rubric","freeze","one case rubric"],["freeze","assess","frozen version + saved run evidence required"],["freeze","semantic","asynchronous human inspection","dashed"],["semantic","revise","explicit finalized changes","dashed"],["revise","assess","new bound version; queue affected runs in both methods","dashed"],["specialists","peers","each critic’s original context and proposal"],["specialists","modelReconcile","original proposals retained"],["integrator","modelReconcile","integration decision history"],["specialists","finalAssembly","preserved proposal text"],["references","assess","optional current guidance","dashed"],["eligible","assess","bound eligible chart evidence"],["assess","semantic","optional review of flagged rubric issues; record exposure","dashed"]],"boundary":"One shared frozen case rubric per authoring condition","note":"Sol is the default author and separate-session grader; shared blind spots remain. Single-author mode is an explicit experiment. Current guidance is separate from the historical case clock. Human review does not delay provisional grading. Any revision applied to saved runs is made after seeing results. Any failed, invalid or unknown authoring step stops dependent authoring for that case; keep its evidence and mark unavailable grades honestly. Encounter simulation continues independently. Public-reference access is configured for all rubric-authoring stages; reference use remains optional. Sol is the default author for the selected team condition; the declared frozen authoring condition, not the preparation method, chooses the shared rubric. Reference edges for the integrator/reconciler are omitted from the drawing but remain optional as described in their pop-ups."},{"id":"evaluation","title":"Store, grade, review and retain the originals","short":"Review & report","subtitle":"Three quality areas; human handoff readability","description":"Store outputs and provisional grades as available. The first-judgment gate changes what a named reviewer sees, not whether grading or dossiers exist. This drawing follows the dossier reviewer. Independent rubric-only review is described in Rubric team; its access/exposure policy still needs declaration before the comparison. Case-wide display rule: this reviewer must judge all required case runs before any method label or AI grade is revealed.","groups":[["stages","Reading order",["earlierOutputs","finalap","rubric","assess","failure","dossier","initial","reveal","revise","regradeQueue","report"]]],"edges":[["earlierOutputs","assess","prepared brief, intake, handoff/alerts + transcripts"],["finalap","assess","final assessment / plan"],["rubric","assess","one frozen rubric for both methods"],["earlierOutputs","dossier","saved stage artifacts, patient packet and SP handouts"],["finalap","dossier","saved final draft"],["rubric","dossier","frozen rubric + authoring record"],["assess","dossier","version-bound feedback stored when available"],["failure","dossier","from every stage: all outcome states","dashed"],["dossier","initial","all case runs; method/grades hidden"],["initial","reveal","this reviewer judged every case run"],["dossier","reveal","method, rubric, available grades"],["reveal","revise","optional after-exposure human edits","dashed"],["rubric","revise","original criteria retained"],["revise","regradeQueue","explicit finalized version"],["regradeQueue","assess","pending BOTH saved methods; old grades kept","dashed"],["dossier","report","same saved evidence; unblinded report"],["initial","dossier","separate judgments for every run","dashed"],["reveal","dossier","case-wide exposure record","dashed"],["revise","dossier","new rubric version and reviewer reasons","dashed"],["regradeQueue","dossier","pending post-hoc jobs; originals retained","dashed"]],"boundary":"Original feedback first; later revisions are made after seeing results","note":"40 runs / 80 conversations / 10 reused cases. Argon is the workflow model: its 8,000-call ceiling includes probes, continuations and one optional separately bound corrected-candidate confirmation. Author/grader/Opus calls have separate ledgers. No overall numerical score, validated usability claim or clinical certification. Judgments remain per run; reveal permission is case-wide and reviewer-specific. Runtime enforcement of this newly adopted requirement is still pending."}]};
